PUBLIC RESPONSE: D. Hool

In the rhymed octosyllabic couplets of the *Book of the Duchess* the four lexical forms wente, here, fool and countour all occur in end-position and are regularly coupled in Chaucer’s rhyme-pairs. 

* wente is the normal past of *gon* and is rhymed, e.g. wente / sente;
* here is a normal couplet end, e.g. here / dere;
* fool is used in the dreamer’s self-mockery and is rhymed in the poem’s couplets, e.g. fool / cool;
* countour, ‘counsellor / counter’, does occur in the poem and is put into rhyme at line-end.

The form hool ≈ hoole / houl does not occur as a rhyme-word in the Duchess-text. The poem never puts a *hool-* end in couplet rhyming position, whereas the other four items are attested as rhymed line-endings.

6.text:
PUBLIC RESPONSE: E. Countour

In the octosyllabic couplets of The Book of the Duchess the five lexical items you list all occur in the poem, but only four of them are ever used as rhyme-endings in the work. 

* wente ~ sente etc. is a routine -ente rhyme in the early narration of the dream;
* here is regularly brought to line-end and rhymed with there / dere / mere in the dialogue passages;
* fool is brought to line-end and is rhymed with cool / pool in the self-deprecating address to the Black Knight;
* hool, ME ‘whole, hale’, is the actual rhyme-partner Chaucer uses for fool in the couplet that closes the Knight’s first lament, fool / hool.

countour, the ME form of ‘counter, counterfeiter, story-teller’, is indeed used in the Duchess, e.g. in the description of the narrator’s telling of the tale, but it is never set up as a rhyme-end in the Book of the Duchess. The rhyme countour ~ tournour / recountour that is familiar from the later works is not exploited in the Duchess, so countour is the item of the list that Chaucer does not make a rhyme with in this poem.

